{
  "title": "Alkaline earth metals",
  "topic": "Alkaline earth metals",
  "turns": [
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0001",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "Teach me about alkaline earth metals\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0002",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "Certainly! 😊 I'll explain it simply, with examples.\nBy ‘alkaline earth metals,’ you mean the Group 2 alkaline earth metals, right?\nWhat are alkaline earth metals?\nThe metals in the second group (Group 2) of the periodic table are called alkaline earth metals.\nAlkali metals, alkaline earth metals, and halogens: Various names for periodic table groups — Let's Start Solid State Science\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\nsolid-mater.com\n\n\nThere are six alkaline earth metals\nElement\nSymbol\n\nBeryllium\nBe\n\nMagnesium\nMg\n\nCalcium\nCa\n\nStrontium\nSr\n\nBarium\nBa\n\nRadium\nRa\n\n\n\n\n\nTheir main properties\nThey have 2 electrons in their outermost shell.\n\n\n\nThey usually form +2 ions.\n\n\n\nThey conduct heat and electricity.\n\n\n\nTheir reactivity generally increases as you move down the group.\n\n\n\nTheir oxides are generally alkaline.\n\n\n\n\nA simple example\nWhen calcium (Ca) reacts with water, calcium hydroxide and hydrogen gas are formed.\nCa+2H2O→Ca(OH)2+H2Ca+2H2​O→Ca(OH)2​+H2​\nNow a question for you:\nWhich group of the periodic table contains the alkaline earth metals?\nGroup 1\nGroup 2\nGroup 3\n\nCheck answer\n\nWhich grade are you in — 9, 10, 11, or 12? I can teach alkaline earth metals as a full lesson suited to your level.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0003",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "Are their physical and chemical properties all the same?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0004",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "No! 😊 The physical and chemical properties of alkaline earth metals are not all identical. But they do share some similarities.\n1. Physical properties\nPhysical properties are characteristics we can observe without changing a substance's chemical identity.\nColor: They are generally silvery white.\n\n\n\nLuster: They have a metallic shine.\n\n\n\nElectrical conductivity: They conduct electricity and heat well.\n\n\n\nDensity: Each metal has a different density.\n\n\n\nMelting point: The metals do not all have the same melting point.\n\n\n\n\n2. Chemical properties\nA chemical property describes how a substance reacts with another substance to form a new one.\nValency: Usually 2.\n\n\n\nReaction with oxygen: They generally form metal oxides.\n\n\n\nReaction with water: Not all the metals react at the same rate.\n\n\n\nReaction with acid: Many alkaline earth metals react with dilute acids and release hydrogen gas.\n\n\n\nReactivity: It generally increases as you move down the group.\n\n\n\n\n3. An important difference\nPhysical properties\nChemical properties\n\nColor\nReaction with oxygen\n\nDensity\nReaction with water\n\nMelting point\nReaction with acid\n\nElectrical conductivity\nFormation of oxides\n\n\n\n\n\nWhat to remember for an exam:\nBecause alkaline earth metals are in the same group, their chemical properties are largely similar. But their physical properties and chemical reactivity vary from element to element.\nA short question: Are the 2 electrons in the outermost shell of alkaline earth metals a physical property or a chemical property?\nIt is not directly either one; it is a feature of atomic structure. It is the main reason their chemical properties are similar.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0005",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "Which properties change, and why?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0006",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "As you move down the alkaline earth metals (Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra), some physical and chemical properties change. Let's look simply at why they change. 😊\n1. Changes in physical properties\nProperty\nMoving down the group\n\nAtomic radius\nIncreases ↑\n\nAtomic mass\nIncreases ↑\n\nDensity\nGenerally increases; there are some exceptions\n\nMelting point\nDoes not change in a regular pattern\n\nIonization energy\nDecreases ↓\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Changes in chemical properties\nProperty\nMoving down the group\n\nReactivity\nGenerally increases ↑\n\nTendency to lose electrons\nIncreases ↑\n\nMetallic character\nIncreases ↑\n\nAlkalinity of oxides\nGenerally increases ↑\n\nAbility to react with water\nIncreases ↑\n\n\n\n\n\n3. Why do these changes occur?\nMain reason: The number of electron shells increases.\nLook at the electron arrangements\nCaptured image\n\nDiagram: The number of electron shells increases from Be to Mg to Ca.\nThis causes three major changes:\nAtomic radius increases: Adding new electron shells makes the atom larger.\n\n\n\nIonization energy decreases: The outer electrons are farther from the nucleus, and inner electrons shield them, so they are easier to remove.\n\n\n\nReactivity increases: Because it becomes easier to lose the two outer electrons, the metals generally become more reactive.\n\n\n\n\nAn important answer for exams\nMoving down the alkaline earth metals, the atomic radius increases as the number of electron shells increases. Greater shielding lowers ionization energy. As a result, the tendency to lose electrons and the reactivity increase.\nRemember:\nAtomic radius ↑ → ionization energy ↓ → reactivity ↑\nWould you like me to explain next, with examples, why the reactions of alkaline earth metals with water vary?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    }
  ],
